00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
plans by the bosnian serb leadership to  exit some of the country's tripartite  institutions including the military are  described by the international  community's high representative  christian schmidt as tantamount to  succession they constitute he says the  greatest existential threat of the  post-war period  he's talking about bosnian serb leader  milorad doddik who says a new army will  be set up in the serb run entity of  republika serpska with the bosnian army  forced to withdraw if necessary he says  the serbs will call on their friends for  help a presumed reference to serbia  proper and russia a republican  the republic of sebsco will control its  own affairs in a legal constitutional  manner including by having its own army  judiciary fiscal administration as well  as intelligence and security agencies we  will re-establish all these institutions  last month a training exercise by  bosnian serb police was denounced by  muslim bosniak and croat leaders the  other members of a three-way power  sharing government formed by the 1995  dayton peace agreement  they took place on the mountain  overlooking sarajevo from where serbs  snipers and artillery laid siege to the  capital beginning in 1992. thousands of  civilians died in a war that culminated  in what international judges have called  genocide when more than 8 000 muslim men  and boys were massacred by serb troops  in the eastern enclave of srebrenica  the high representatives report to the  un this week says there is a very real  prospect of a return to conflict in  bosnia but there is no obvious appetite  on the part of western powers to  increase their security presence there  political tensions are part of daily  life in a country such as boston  herzegovina where you have different  groups competing for political power  but you did never see threats  uh off be it violence or be it secession  or be it parallel institutions come this  far as they have now at least not in a  very long time  milorad doddik has for years called for  the separation of the serb run part of  bosnia from the rest of the country  just weeks ago dodeck shared these  scenes on social media he and colleagues  singing serb nationalist folk songs in  their sarajevo headquarters it was a  reminder that the u.s sponsored peace in  bosnia did not bring ethnic harmony nor  did it come with any long-term guarantee  jonah al jazeera
 

